k and Silver.\n","*   Automatic transmission cars are significantly more frequent than Manual.\n","*   Gasoline is the most common fuel type.\n","*   Sedans are the most represented car model in the dataset.\n","*   The North American region has the highest number of entries in the dataset.\n","*   The dataset includes cars ranging from year 2015 to 2023, with a peak around 2020-2022.\n","*   Engine sizes primarily range between 1.5L and 2.5L, with some vehicles having larger engines.\n","*   Mileage shows a wide distribution, with a concentration of lower mileage cars and a spread of higher mileage vehicles.\n","*   Car prices are varied, with a significant number of vehicles priced under \\$30,000, and a long tail extending to higher prices.\n","*   Sales Volume is concentrated at lower values, with some instances of higher sales volume.\n","*   There is a strong negative correlation (-0.97) between 'Year' and 'Mileage\\_KM', indicating that newer cars tend to have lower mileage.\n","*   'Engine\\_Size\\_L' shows a moderate positive correlation (0.67) with 'Price\\_USD'.\n","*   'Price\\_USD' has a moderate negative correlation (-0.51) with 'Mileage\\_KM'.\n","*   'Price\\_USD' shows a weak positive correlation (0.31) with 'Sales\\_Volume'.\n","*   Sales Volume varies significantly across different car models, with some models showing higher median sales and wider ranges.\n","*   Sales Volume distribution differs between regions, with North America showing a higher median and range compared to Europe and Asia.\n","*   Sales Volume distributions are similar between Gasoline and Electric fuel types, while Diesel shows a different pattern.\n","*   Car prices vary considerably by model, year, and region.\n","\n","### Insights or Next Steps\n","\n","*   Investigate the specific car models, regions, and fuel types that show the highest median sales volume to understand factors contributing to their success.\n","*   Explore potential outliers identified in the boxplots f